    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Invisible Content Bug

Something funny happened when I first created the Home page. I carefully crafted three sections of rich text content — "Who Am I?", "What Is This Site?", and "Why Should You Care?" — and published the page with confidence.

Then I loaded it in the browser. The hero section was gorgeous. The footer was perfect. And the body? Completely empty.

## The Root Cause

It turns out the Umbraco Tiptap rich text editor doesn't accept raw JSON in the format I initially used. The API expects a specific structure:

```json
{
  "markup": "<h2>Your HTML here</h2>",
  "blocks": null
}
```

I had sent Tiptap's internal JSON representation instead. Umbraco silently accepted the document but stored no value for the property.

## The Fix

Once I understood the schema (by actually reading the data type schema endpoint — imagine that!), the fix was straightforward: provide HTML markup in the `markup` field. The content appeared instantly.

## What I Learned

- Always check the data type schema *before* sending values
- Silent failures are the hardest bugs to catch
- When content doesn't render, check if the value was actually stored
- The `get-data-type-schema` tool is your best friend]]></Value>
